# Dialog Standards

All dialogs and modals MUST have solid, opaque backgrounds. Translucent dialogs make content unreadable and create poor UX.

## Critical Rules

### 1. NEVER Use Translucent/Transparent Backgrounds

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Solid background (uses bg-background or bg-card)
<DialogContent size="md">
  <DialogHeader>
    <DialogTitle>Dialog Title</DialogTitle>
  </DialogHeader>
  <div>Content</div>
</DialogContent>

// ❌ WRONG - Translucent background
<DialogContent className="bg-background/80"> // Wrong - translucent
<DialogContent className="bg-white/90"> // Wrong - translucent
<DialogContent className="backdrop-blur-xl bg-background/50"> // Wrong - translucent
```

### 2. Dialog Content MUST Have Solid Background

The `DialogContent` component already provides `bg-background` which is solid. **Do NOT override this with opacity values.**

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Uses default solid background
<DialogContent size="lg" className="max-h-[80vh]">
  {/* Content */}
</DialogContent>

// ✅ CORRECT - Can add bg-card if needed for contrast
<DialogContent className="bg-card">
  {/* Content */}
</DialogContent>

// ❌ WRONG - Adding transparency
<DialogContent className="bg-background/95"> // Wrong
<DialogContent className="bg-opacity-90"> // Wrong
<DialogContent style={{ opacity: 0.9 }}> // Wrong
```

### 3. Overlay Backdrop Transparency

The dialog overlay (dark background behind dialog) uses semi-transparent dark background (NO blur) to allow seeing the page behind while keeping focus on the modal.

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Overlay is semi-transparent (already default in dialog.tsx)
// Uses: bg-black/40 (40% opacity, no backdrop-blur)

// Dialog content is ALWAYS solid
<DialogContent className="bg-background"> // Solid, opaque

// ❌ WRONG - Do NOT add backdrop-blur (causes fuzzy appearance)
<DialogOverlay className="backdrop-blur-sm"> // Wrong
```

**Key principles:**
- Overlay: `bg-black/40` (semi-transparent, allows seeing page)
- Modal content: `bg-background` (solid, opaque)
- NO `backdrop-blur` anywhere (causes fuzzy appearance)

### 4. Card Backgrounds in Dialogs

Cards within dialogs should also be solid:

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Solid card backgrounds
<Card className="bg-violet-50 dark:bg-violet-950/30">
  {/* The /30 opacity is acceptable for ACCENT backgrounds */}
  {/* as long as the parent DialogContent is solid */}
</Card>

// ✅ CORRECT - Solid card
<Card className="bg-card">
  {/* Content */}
</Card>

// ❌ WRONG - Translucent card in dialog
<Card className="bg-white/50"> // Too transparent
```

## Use `size` Prop, Not Custom Max-Width

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Use size prop
<DialogContent size="sm">   // 425px
<DialogContent size="md">   // 600px
<DialogContent size="lg">   // 800px

// ❌ WRONG - Custom widths
<DialogContent className="max-w-4xl"> // Use size prop instead
<DialogContent className="sm:max-w-[900px]"> // Use size prop
```

**Exception:** If you need a width not covered by size variants (like 4xl), you can use custom width BUT still must ensure solid background:

```tsx
// ✅ ACCEPTABLE - Custom width for special cases
<DialogContent className="max-w-4xl !bg-background">
  {/* Explicit solid background with !important */}
</DialogContent>
```

## Wide Dialogs with Horizontal Scrolling

For comparison tables or wide content that needs horizontal scrolling:

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Responsive wide dialog
<DialogContent className="max-w-[95vw] lg:max-w-7xl max-h-[80vh] flex flex-col p-0 !bg-background">
  <DialogHeader className="border-b flex-shrink-0">
    <DialogTitle>Compare Items</DialogTitle>
  </DialogHeader>
  
  <ScrollArea className="flex-1">
    <div className="p-6 min-w-max">
      <table className="w-full">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            {/* Sticky first column */}
            <th className="sticky left-0 bg-background z-10">Attribute</th>
            <th>Item 1</th>
            <th>Item 2</th>
            <th>Item 3</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ScrollBar orientation="horizontal" />
  </ScrollArea>
</DialogContent>
```

**Key patterns:**
- Use `max-w-[95vw]` for responsive width (prevents breaking mobile)
- Use `lg:max-w-7xl` for wide desktop dialogs
- Wrap content in `min-w-max` to allow table to grow
- Make first column `sticky left-0 bg-background z-10`
- Include horizontal ScrollBar

## Dialog Stacking (z-index)

Dialogs should use consistent z-index values:

```tsx
// Default dialog z-index (already in dialog.tsx)
z-50 // Dialog content
z-50 // Dialog overlay

// If you need a dialog on top of another dialog
<DialogContent className="z-[60]">
```

## Full Checklist

Before committing any dialog:

- [ ] Dialog content has solid background (no opacity < 100%)
- [ ] No `bg-opacity-*` or `/XX` opacity modifiers on dialog content
- [ ] Uses `size` prop when possible (sm/md/lg)
- [ ] Cards within dialog have solid or acceptable dark-mode backgrounds
- [ ] No inline styles setting opacity on dialog content
- [ ] Overlay can have transparency (already default)
- [ ] Content is readable against all backgrounds

## Common Violations to Fix

```tsx
// ❌ VIOLATION 1: Custom width without solid background enforcement
<DialogContent className="max-w-4xl max-h-[80vh] flex flex-col p-0">
  
// ✅ FIX: Explicitly ensure solid background
<DialogContent size="lg" className="max-h-[80vh] flex flex-col p-0 gap-0 bg-background">

// ❌ VIOLATION 2: Sheet with translucent background
<SheetContent className="bg-white/90 backdrop-blur">

// ✅ FIX: Use solid background
<SheetContent className="bg-background">

// ❌ VIOLATION 3: Popover with transparency
<PopoverContent className="bg-popover/80">

// ✅ FIX: Use solid background
<PopoverContent className="bg-popover">
```

## Why This Matters

1. **Readability**: Translucent dialogs make text hard to read
2. **Accessibility**: Poor contrast for users with vision impairments
3. **Professionalism**: Translucent dialogs look unfinished/buggy
4. **Focus**: Solid backgrounds help users focus on dialog content
5. **Consistency**: All dialogs should behave the same way

## Related Components

This rule applies to:

- `Dialog` / `DialogContent`
- `Sheet` / `SheetContent`
- `Popover` / `PopoverContent`
- `AlertDialog` / `AlertDialogContent`
- `DropdownMenu` / `DropdownMenuContent` (already solid by default)
- `HoverCard` / `HoverCardContent`
- Any modal or overlay component

## Testing

To verify your dialog is solid:

1. Open the dialog
2. Check if you can see page content through the dialog
3. If yes → dialog is translucent (bad)
4. If no → dialog is solid (good)

**Visual Test:**
- Dialog content should completely obscure whatever is behind it
- Only the overlay backdrop should show page content (dimmed/blurred)

## Why These Regressions Keep Happening

**Root Causes:**

1. **CSS Variable Opacity Issues**: Tailwind CSS variables like `bg-popover`, `bg-background`, `bg-card` sometimes don't compile to solid colors in production builds. This appears to be a Next.js + Tailwind compilation edge case.

2. **Pattern Copying**: When developers copy code from one component to another, they propagate translucent backgrounds (`bg-white/90`, `backdrop-blur-sm`).

3. **"Frosted Glass" Aesthetic**: The translucent look seems modern, but it makes content unreadable and is poor UX.

4. **Missing Testing**: Visual regressions aren't caught until manual testing in production.

**Prevention Strategy:**

1. **Always use `!important` for overlay backgrounds**: `!bg-white` not `bg-background`
2. **Check before committing**: Look for `/` in any bg-* class (indicates opacity)
3. **Test in production**: Check deployed site with hard refresh, not just localhost
4. **Audit regularly**: Search codebase for `bg-.*\/[0-9]` patterns

## Automated Check

Before committing dialogs, run:

```bash
# Find translucent backgrounds in UI components
grep -r "bg-.*\/[0-9]" src/components/ui/ --include="*.tsx"

# Find backdrop-blur without solid backgrounds
grep -r "backdrop-blur" src/components/ui/ --include="*.tsx"
```

If results found → fix before committing.
